  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The problem is to minimize the maximum overlap integral for a step function h under given constraints. The previous approach used coordinate descent which is prone to local minima. We propose switching to a particle swarm optimization (PSO) approach to better explore the solution space and escape local optima. PSO is suitable for non-convex problems and allows for global search through swarm dynamics.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Variables:\n   - h: n_points-dimensional vector, h \u2208 [0, 1]^n_points\n   - Integral constraint: sum(h) * dx = 1 \u2192 sum(h) = n_points / 2\n   - Objective: C\u2085 = max_k (h \u22c6 (1 - h))[k] * dx, where \u22c6 is cross-correlation\n   Fitness function for PSO particles = C\u2085\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialization\n      - One particle from initial_h_values (if available)\n      - Remaining particles randomized with structured patterns (e.g.,segments, sawtooth)\n   b. PSO loop:\n      - For each particle:\n          i. Compute fitness (C\u2085)\n          ii. Update personal best\n      - Update swarm best\n      - Update particle velocities and positions with PSO formulas\n      - Project positions onto feasible region (clip and scale)\n   c. Adaptive mechanisms:\n      - Inertia weight that decreases over time\n      - Local search around personal best when stagnation detected\n   d. Termination when budget exhausted or negligible improvement\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - n_points: 100 (same as parent for compatibility)\n   - Swarm size: 50 particles (balance exploration vs. resources)\n   - Inertia weight: starts at 0.9, decreases to 0.4 linearly\n   - Cognitive/social coefficients: 1.5 each (standard for PSO)\n   - Velocity limits: \u00b10.1 to prevent overshooting\n   - Perturbation strategy: random step around personal best on stagnation\n   - Multi-init strategies: mix structured and random starts\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Iterations limited by time budget, with runtime checks\n   -Fallback: initial_h_values if no improvement found\n   -Robustness: particle projections ensure feasibility, velocity limits prevent divergence\n\n6. Validation and return\n   -Ensure all particles are feasible before fitness eval\n   -Final solution must pass feasibility checks\n   - Small sample test: verify constraints on a small n_points case",
